{
  "requirement": "Usage MUST record mandatory input counts and sizes by evidence kind, in UTF-8\nbytes and lines, and the same information for additional inputs requested through\n`habeas` or `ripgrep`. A requirements input MUST also record its number of matched declarations.\nUsage MUST distinguish unique inputs from repeated reads, successful from\nfailed reads, and prepared inputs from those actually sent to the model.\nFile-discovery calls MUST count as tool calls without recording directory\nlistings as evidence or source inputs.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "## Judgment: `requirements.authoring.usage_inputs` is satisfied\n\nI judge that the implementation meets every obligation and that the tests cover the main cases. The one weakness is minor and noted under Testing.\n\n### Implementation\n\n**Counts and sizes by evidence kind, for mandatory and additional inputs**\n- `UsageLog::prepared` (`src/authoring/usage.rs:142`) pushes one `InputUsage` entry (`usage.rs:42`) for each input.\n- Each entry carries `origin`, `kind`, `bytes`, `lines` and `components`.\n- `origin` is `\"mandatory\"`, `\"habeas\"` or `\"ripgrep\"`, so mandatory and additional inputs are separate.\n- `kind` is `outline`, `requirements` or `definition`. A fourth value, `builtin`, covers a built-in's fixed text; I take it to be harmless support for built-in text.\n- `bytes` is the UTF-8 length of the text lines plus the joining newlines. `lines` is the number of text lines. The built-in text is a single joined paragraph, so its line count is right.\n- `converse` (`src/authoring/mod.rs:855`) calls `usage.prepared(<tool name>, &delivery.inputs)` for every successful `habeas` or `ripgrep` delivery.\n- `assess_subject` and `followup_sources` call `usage.prepared(\"mandatory\", …)` for the first, second and verification conversations, each with its own log.\n\n**Matched declarations for a requirements input**\n- `components` is the length of the `matches` array, and is 1 for the other kinds.\n\n**Unique inputs versus repeated reads**\n- `unique` comes from `seen.insert(input.to_string())`, a set shared across the log.\n- A repeat of a mandatory input or an earlier read is recorded with `unique: false`.\n\n**Successful versus failed reads**\n- `UsageLog::tool(name, failed)` increments `tool_calls` and, on failure, `tool_failures`, per tool.\n- `converse` passes `failed = true` for tool errors and for submission errors.\n- `skipped_tool` records skipped calls separately.\n- `tool_name` stops model-controlled names leaking into the log by mapping unknown names to `\"unknown\"`.\n\n**Prepared versus actually sent**\n- `begin_request` stamps each prepared input with `submitted_in_request`.\n- `end_request` stamps `sent_in_request` only when the response carried usage, which means the request succeeded.\n- A failed or throttled request therefore leaves an input submitted but not sent, until a retry succeeds.\n\n**File discovery counts as a tool call but is not an input**\n- `converse` calls `usage.tool` for every dispatched call, including `list_files`.\n- `list_files` returns `inputs: Vec::new()` (`tools.rs:170`), so `prepared` never records a listing.\n- `requirements.authoring.file_discovery` states \"Directory listings MUST NOT become evidence inputs\", and its text carries that part out.\n\n**Marking.** The `{impl}` markers cover `prepared`, `begin_request`, `end_request`, `tool`, `skipped_tool`, `tool_name`, `InputUsage`, `converse`, `assess_subject`, `followup_sources` and `configured_record`. I found no behaviour in the marked code beyond what the requirement and the sibling usage requirements specify.\n\n### Testing\n\n- **`input_metadata_counts_components_and_reads_without_persisting_source`** (`usage.rs:379`) covers the following:\n  - the `outline` kind;\n  - byte counts for multibyte text (`λ`) and line counts;\n  - `components == 2` for a requirements input;\n  - `unique` true on first use and false on a repeat, with `origin: \"habeas\"`;\n  - `submitted_in_request` and `sent_in_request` moving from null to set;\n  - no source text or names in the stored log.\n- **`failed_submission_can_be_confirmed_on_a_later_attempt`** (`usage.rs:638`) shows a failed request leaves `sent_in_request` null, and a later success sets it while `submitted_in_request` stays at the first attempt.\n- **`throttled_requests_retry_unchanged_and_record_every_attempt`** (`mod.rs:1255`) checks `submitted_in_request == 0` and `sent_in_request == 8` after eight failed attempts.\n- **`tool_outcomes_are_distinct_and_unknown_names_do_not_leak_model_text`** (`usage.rs:607`) covers calls, failures and skips per tool, and the `unknown` bucket.\n- **The CLI test** (`tests/authoring_cli.rs:235`) checks the following end to end:\n  - `list_files` counts as a call but appears in no input origin;\n  - `ripgrep` inputs have non-zero bytes and lines;\n  - `habeas` inputs are unique on first read and non-unique on repeat, with `sent_in_request == 1`;\n  - one failed `habeas` read is counted in `tool_failures`;\n  - the mandatory `definition` kind has non-zero bytes;\n  - the log contains no source, rationale or credential content.\n\nNot asserted directly: the `requirements` kind label on the unit-test input, which is exercised only through `components`. This does not leave a meaningful gap.",
  "references": "dc43fb7d9d6f3d57673af4f00f38c1477c5deda399f79d1bccb7a3770e5c7190",
